n time before winter accelerates  covid19 infection rates once again  for more let's talk to simon clark he's  a microbiologist at the university of  reading in the uk  tell us what you think about this new  possible  breakthrough treatment  i think it's a very exciting treatment a  very exciting and interesting way of  administering an ability for doctors to  block the virus from replicating and  therefore causing a more severe  infection so we don't have a huge amount  of antiviral medicines compared to sort  of more traditional antibiotics which of  course do not work against  viral infections so this is a a very  exciting very interesting uh new therapy  which should be i would expect useful  against other infections as well  can you tell us exactly how it works how  it manages to block the virus  so when the virus is replicated inside  our cells because our cells  act like mini factories for making new  virus  this chemical introduces changes to the  genetic code and that stops it being  replicated basically  and if you can't produce more genetic  code more genetic material you can't  have more virus  so  that's how it works and like i say i  expect it will work with other things we  hear that it was originally  developed for treatment of flu but i  think it's a it's got potential other  applications as well  what about the time window within which  it has to be applied to the patient i  know it can't be used too early or too  late can it  no they have to have what is called mild  to moderate symptoms um and uh we're not  exactly sure what that means uh but  there is a fairly it seems a fairly  tight window if you if you give it to  somebody too late then they're not going  to be  to be able to benefit from it and if you  give it to them too early that could  cause problems as well  it really does  depend on getting the the timing right  and we've seen that with other  treatments as well the steroid  treatments that we see which also  reduced  the numbers of de